    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d159">

                <pb facs="0339" n="211" xml:id="pg211"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 159.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Scruggs</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-05-16"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 16,
                        1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 36.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 182, of December 26 last, touching the abuses of American citizenship, and
                    in which you cite the case of Aurelio F. Pinzon, a native and resident of
                    Colombia, who claims to be a naturalized citizen of the United States, and as
                    such has appealed to you for protection.</p>
                <p>On Mr. Pinzon’s own statement the Government of the United States is under no
                    obligation to protect him as against the country of his original allegiance, his
                    certificate of naturalization having been admittedly obtained in fraud of the
                    United States statutes.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. F. BAYARD.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
